ISOLATED INSTALLATION AND ENCRYPTED RECOVERY ACCEPTANCE
=====================================================

FreeSDN remains Community / Early Access. This rehearsal checks a bounded Lite
deployment; it does not certify a customer upgrade or an enterprise release.

Installer security
------------------
install.sh creates .env.lite/.env.pro/.env.max/.env.dev with random credentials
and verified mode 0600. It preserves existing credentials when rerun, including
SECRET_KEY, ENCRYPTION_SALT, JWT_SIGNING_KEY and SLA_REPORT_DELIVERY_EPOCH. Existing
files must be owned by the invoking account, regular and singly linked. Symlinks,
hard links, special files and filesystems that cannot enforce private permissions
are refused. With WSL, install on its Linux filesystem if the Windows mount does
not support POSIX permissions. Do not make secrets world-readable as a workaround.

--domain accepts a DNS hostname without scheme/port/path; --email also requires
--domain. Invalid arguments are refused before image build or service startup.
The chosen HTTPS origin sets both PUBLIC_BASE_URL and FRONTEND_URL. This does not
enable report mail, create an epoch, configure DNS or provide a valid backup key.
Backup encryption still requires an independently retained private key. The
installer does not generate or pretend to validate that operator recovery key.

Production cookies remain Secure. Access over a LAN address must use HTTPS with
a certificate trusted by the client. Lite's local HTTP mode is not approval to
send credentials over a remote unencrypted connection. Use --domain with valid
DNS and TLS, or provision a trusted private certificate/reverse proxy. Never
disable Secure cookies or TLS verification to make a remote login appear to work.

Run the rehearsal
-----------------
Use Linux amd64, Docker Engine/Compose, Python 3 and already-built candidate
backend, PostgreSQL, TimescaleDB, Valkey and Caddy application images. Run against
a reviewed public source export, never a live installation directory:

    python3 scripts/check_install_acceptance.py \
      --source /absolute/path/to/reviewed-public-source \
      --backend CANDIDATE_BACKEND --postgres CANDIDATE_POSTGRES \
      --logdb CANDIDATE_LOGDB --valkey CANDIDATE_VALKEY --edge CANDIDATE_CADDY \
      --work-dir /absolute/new/private/acceptance-run

The directory must not already exist and must support 0700. Images are resolved
once to exact IDs. No images are pulled by the runner. The runner creates its own
random projects, internal networks and named volumes, with no published ports.
It removes builds and host port mappings from the resolved shipped Lite Compose
plan, disables automatic restarts, uses monitor-only adapter mode and chooses
Caddy's localhost TLS mode. The HTTP probe trusts only that fixture's private CA
and verifies certificate hostname/TLS; it does not set verify=False. Only reviewed
source configuration files and the disposable public backup key are host-mounted.

The test exercises real service startup under Lite limits; actual edge/API setup
and anonymous setup refusal; Secure cookie authentication and CSRF enforcement;
dashboard reads; CSV/PDF generation/download; a live Celery worker and all five
queues; container recreation; backup refusal without encryption; the actual GPG
backup pipeline without unused anonymous database volumes; offline restoration to fresh database and application volumes;
Timescale event content, original credential ciphertext and archive fidelity;
and denial of old JWTs after an independent signing-key cutover. Application
writers are stopped before backup; every source service is stopped before target
writers start. Vault keys stay unchanged. pg_dump changes search_path, so the
Timescale pre/post-restore functions are explicitly schema-qualified.

Only report.json is designed for sharing. Keep every other file private: the
directory contains configuration credentials, tokens, backup decryption keys,
database dumps, file archives and raw logs. Synthetic keys are intentionally
disposable test material, not a model for production private-key storage.
On failure, inspect its partial report and private logs. The runner removes only
its own temporary resources and reports cleanup failures; it never prunes Docker.
Retain original deployment volumes, images and backups separately.

What a pass does NOT establish
-----------------------------
- Same-candidate recreation is not a legacy-version/database-runtime upgrade.
- Recovery deliberately starts an empty broker. Customer jobs, reserved work,
  HA credentials, primary election, failover and device-side reconciliation are
  separate gates in redis/HA-UPGRADING, QUEUE-INVENTORY and QUEUE-BACKUP-RECOVERY.
- This does not preserve arbitrary PostgreSQL roles/ACLs using the scheduled
  --no-owner/--no-privileges dumps. Custom role migration needs separate acceptance.
- No original hardware, external SMTP, public ACME, VPN or browser-rendered UI is
  used. The report-mail epoch has its own check_report_recovery.py rehearsal.
- The dataset and timings are bounded synthetic evidence, not production fleet
  capacity, sustained soak, retention, host-loss protection or RTO/RPO promises.
- Published-image installation, hosted CI, signing and release support remain
  separate acceptance requirements. Do not label this an enterprise certification.
